{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' candidate.py\"","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n# Frozen full pooled source below; final Model appends the controlled fusion.\n# Standalone resolution-factorial module. The original gap-parent source\n# below is preserved (class renamed); the final Model supplies the new readout.\n\"\"\"Elapsed-time localization of adjacent category/brand recurrence.\n\nThe exact chronological-product parent is retained as _ParentModel. A new\nzero-initialized residual reads gap-conditioned transition counts. The 2x2\nstudy replaces gap/recurrence association and category/brand joint equality\nseparately. All modes execute the same tables and allocate equal parameters.\nHistories are host-supplied causal prefixes; no items or extra events exist.\n\"\"\"\nimport math\nimport torch\nfrom torch import nn\n\nCONFIG = {'embedding_dim': 16, 'hidden': [128, 64], 'lr': 0.001, 'joint_mode': 'observed', 'statistic_mode': 'count', 'grouping_mode': 'match', 'histogram_grouping': 'match', 'histogram_pairing': 'observed', 'moment_grouping': 'match', 'moment_pairing': 'independent', 'field_mode': 'contrast', 'field_grouping': 'rank', 'field_scope': 'global', 'field_layout': 'compact', 'global_direction': 'norm_collapsed', 'identity_mode': 'joint', 'context_mode': 'joint', 'repeat_mode': 'joint', 'overlap_mode': 'observed', 'transition_order': 'chronological', 'transition_pairing': 'product', 'gap_mode': 'observed', 'gap_pairing': 'product', 'gap_resolution': 'global', 'gap_channels': 'all', 'product_scope': 'pooled', 'gap_association': 'observed', 'fusion_mode': 'target', 'fusion_hidden': 32, 'readout_scale_mode': 'raw'}\n\n\nclass _ParentModel(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, meta, config=None):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.config = {**CONFIG, **(config or {})}\n        self.joint_mode = self.config['joint_mode']\n        self.statistic_mode = self.config['statistic_mode']\n        self.grouping_mode = self.config['grouping_mode']\n        if self.joint_mode not in ('observed', 'independent'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown joint_mode')\n        if self.statistic_mode not in ('count', 'rate'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown statistic_mode')\n        if self.grouping_mode not in ('match', 'rank', 'occupied_global'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown grouping_mode')\n        # Parent means, histograms and independent moments stay fixed in\n        # true-match groups; only the appended field readout is intervened on.\n        if self.grouping_mode != 'match':\n            raise ValueError('This study fixes learned pooling to match')\n        self.histogram_grouping = self.config['histogram_grouping']\n        self.histogram_pairing = self.config['histogram_pairing']\n        if self.histogram_grouping not in ('match', 'rank'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown histogram_grouping')\n        if self.histogram_pairing not in ('observed', 'independent'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown histogram_pairing')\n        self.moment_grouping = self.config['moment_grouping']\n        self.moment_pairing = self.config['moment_pairing']\n        if self.moment_grouping not in ('match', 'rank'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown moment_grouping')\n        if self.moment_pairing not in ('observed', 'independent'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown moment_pairing')\n        if self.histogram_grouping != 'match' or self.histogram_pairing != 'observed':\n            raise ValueError('Incumbent histogram must remain observed/match')\n        self.field_mode = self.config['field_mode']\n        self.field_grouping = self.config['field_grouping']\n        if self.field_mode not in ('contrast', 'norm_collapsed'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown field_mode')\n        if self.field_grouping not in ('match', 'rank'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown field_grouping')\n        if self.moment_grouping != 'match' or self.moment_pairing != 'independent':\n            raise ValueError('This study fixes incumbent independent/match moments')\n        self.field_scope = self.config['field_scope']\n        self.field_layout = self.config['field_layout']\n        self.global_direction = self.config['global_direction']\n        if self.field_scope not in ('rank', 'global'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown field_scope')\n        if self.field_layout not in ('wide', 'compact'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown field_layout')\n        if self.global_direction not in ('contrast', 'norm_collapsed'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown global_direction')\n        if self.field_scope == 'rank' and (self.field_layout != 'wide' or\n                                         self.global_direction != 'contrast'):\n            raise ValueError('Shared reference must retain original rank readout')\n        self.category = meta['category_field_index']\n        self.brand = meta['brand_field_index']\n        dim = self.config['embedding_dim']\n        # Exact ancestor allocation up to the wider output layer. Dormant\n        # attention is retained in every variant; it is never executed.\n        self.embeddings = nn.ModuleList([\n            nn.Embedding(size, dim, padding_idx=0) for size in meta['buckets']\n        ])\n        self.action = nn.Embedding(5, dim, padding_idx=0)\n        self.recency = nn.Embedding(33, dim, padding_idx=0)\n        self.attention = nn.Sequential(\n            nn.Linear(dim * 4, 64), nn.PReLU(), nn.Linear(64, 1)\n        )\n        layers = []\n        field_width = (12 if self.field_layout == 'wide' else 3) * dim\n        width = len(meta['fields']) * dim + 4 * dim + 4 + 64 + 4 * dim + field_width\n        for hidden in self.config['hidden']:\n            layers.extend([nn.Linear(width, hidden), nn.ReLU()])\n            width = hidden\n        layers.append(nn.Linear(width, 1))\n        self.output = nn.Sequential(*layers)\n        # Append allocation AFTER every incumbent parameter. The old parameter\n        # shapes, allocation order and scratch initialization are unchanged.\n        self.identity_mode = self.config['identity_mode']\n        if self.identity_mode not in ('joint', 'independent', 'marginal', 'zero'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown identity_mode')\n        if (self.field_scope, self.field_layout, self.global_direction) != ('global', 'compact', 'norm_collapsed'):\n            raise ValueError('This study fixes the incumbent representation')\n        self.category_vocab = int(meta['buckets'][self.category])\n        self.brand_vocab = int(meta['buckets'][self.brand])\n        # Pair keys use the supplied categorical bucket identities, not raw\n        # products/items. Bound the packed int64 key before any tensor math.\n        if self.category_vocab * self.brand_vocab > (2**63 - 1) // 4:\n            raise ValueError('Packed identity key would overflow int64')\n        self.identity_head = nn.Sequential(\n            nn.Linear(28, 16, bias=False), nn.ReLU(),\n            nn.Linear(16, 1, bias=False)\n        )\n        # A zero final weight preserves the incumbent initial function. In the\n        # zero-input cell the whole bias-free branch stays identically zero.\n        nn.init.zeros_(self.identity_head[-1].weight)\n        # Allocate only AFTER every incumbent parameter. Each registered cell\n        # computes identical empirical and product alternatives before readout.\n        self.context_mode = self.config['context_mode']\n        if self.context_mode not in ('joint', 'independent', 'marginal', 'zero'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown context_mode')\n        if self.identity_mode != 'joint':\n            raise ValueError('This study retains the joint-identity incumbent')\n        if self.category_vocab * self.brand_vocab > (2**63 - 1) // 64:\n            raise ValueError('Packed identity-context key would overflow int64')\n        self.context_head = nn.Sequential(\n            nn.Linear(28, 16, bias=False), nn.ReLU(),\n            nn.Linear(16, 1, bias=False)\n        )\n        nn.init.zeros_(self.context_head[-1].weight)\n\n        # Append after EVERY parent parameter so old shapes, allocation order\n        # and scratch initialization remain identical. Modes allocate equally.\n        self.repeat_mode = self.config['repeat_mode']\n        if self.repeat_mode not in ('joint', 'independent', 'unlabeled', 'zero'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown repeat_mode')\n        if self.context_mode != 'joint':\n            raise ValueError('This study retains coupled_context exactly')\n        self.repeat_head = nn.Sequential(\n            nn.Linear(192, 16, bias=False), nn.ReLU(),\n            nn.Linear(16, 1, bias=False)\n        )\n        nn.init.zeros_(self.repeat_head[-1].weight)\n\n        # Allocate only after ALL parent parameters; every mode is identical.\n        self.overlap_mode = self.config['overlap_mode']\n        if self.overlap_mode not in ('observed', 'product', 'unlabeled', 'zero'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown overlap_mode')\n        if self.repeat_mode != 'joint':\n            raise ValueError('Retain the localized-repeat parent in every cell')\n        self.overlap_head = nn.Sequential(\n            nn.Linear(480, 16, bias=False), nn.ReLU(),\n            nn.Linear(16, 1, bias=False)\n        )\n        nn.init.zeros_(self.overlap_head[-1].weight)\n        # Fixed upper-triangle readout: four groups times C(16,2) = 480.\n        self.register_buffer('overlap_rows', torch.tensor(\n            [i for i in range(16) for j in range(i + 1, 16)], dtype=torch.long), persistent=False)\n        self.register_buffer('overlap_cols', torch.tensor(\n            [j for i in range(16) for j in range(i + 1, 16)], dtype=torch.long), persistent=False)\n\n        # All parent allocations and initialization precede this append.\n        self.transition_order = self.config['transition_order']\n        self.transition_pairing = self.config['transition_pairing']\n        if self.transition_order not in ('chronological', 'exchangeable'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown transition_order')\n        if self.transition_pairing not in ('observed', 'product'):\n            raise ValueError('Unknown transition_pairing')\n        if self.overlap_mode != 'observed':\n            raise ValueError('Retain the entire observed-overlap parent')\n        self.transition_head = nn.Sequential(\n            nn.Linear(1024, 16, bias=False), nn.ReLU(),\n            nn.Linear(16, 1, bias=False)\n        )\n        nn.init.zeros_(self.transition_head[-1].weight)\n\n    def _match_counts(self, batch):\n            mask = batch['history_mask']\n            target_c = batch['static'][:, self.category:self.category + 1]\n            target_b = batch['static'][:, self.brand:self.brand + 1]\n            hc = batch['history_category']\n            hb = batch['history_brand']\n            # Zero represents padding/missing; missing-to-missing is never a match.\n            cm = mask & (target_c > 0) & (hc > 0) & (hc == target_c)\n            bm = mask & (target_b > 0) & (hb > 0) & (hb == target_b)\n            n = mask.float().sum(-1)\n            c = cm.float().sum(-1)\n            b = bm.float().sum(-1)\n            joint = (cm & bm).float().sum(-1)\n            independent = c * b / n.clamp_min(1)\n            return n, c, b, joint, independent\n\n    def _statistics(self, counts):\n            n, c, b, joint, independent = counts\n            selected = joint if self.joint_mode == 'observed' else independent\n            if self.statistic_mode == 'count':\n                # Fixed scale from frozen history_limit=128, never from validation.\n                # For fractional independence estimates the same log1p applies.\n                return torch.log1p(torch.stack([n, c, b, selected], -1)) / math.log(129.0)\n            denom = n.clamp_min(1)\n            return torch.stack([\n                (n > 0).float(), c / denom, b / denom, selected / denom\n            ], -1)\n\n    def _membership(self, batch):\n        mask = batch['history_mask']\n        target_c = batch['static'][:, self.category:self.category + 1]\n        target_b = batch['static'][:, self.brand:self.brand + 1]\n        hc, hb = batch['history_category'], batch['history_brand']\n        cm = mask & (target_c > 0) & (hc > 0) & (hc == target_c)\n        bm = mask & (target_b > 0) & (hb > 0) & (hb == target_b)\n        semantic = torch.stack([\n            mask & ~cm & ~bm, cm & ~bm, bm & ~cm, cm & bm\n        ], dim=1).float()  # [B,4,L]\n        sizes = semantic.sum(-1)\n        if self.grouping_mode == 'rank':\n            # Assign in supplied input order, counting only valid positions.\n            # Prefix thresholds reproduce true cardinalities even with gaps.\n            # This is a positional replacement, not a randomized control.\n            rank = mask.long().cumsum(-1) - 1\n            upper = sizes.long().cumsum(-1)\n            lower = upper - sizes.long()\n            membership = ((rank[:, None, :] >= lower[:, :, None]) &\n                          (rank[:, None, :] < upper[:, :, None]) &\n                          mask[:, None, :]).float()\n        else:\n            membership = semantic\n        return membership, semantic, sizes\n\n    def _represent(self, batch):\n        fields = [embedding(batch['static'][:, i])\n                  for i, embedding in enumerate(self.embeddings)]\n        static = torch.cat(fields, dim=-1)\n        membership, semantic, sizes = self._membership(batch)\n        mask = batch['history_mask']\n        if mask.shape[1] == 0:\n            pools = static.new_zeros((static.shape[0], 4, self.config['embedding_dim']))\n            global_mean = pools[:, 0, :]\n        else:\n            ages = (torch.log2(batch['history_age_seconds'] + 1).long() + 1).clamp(0, 32)\n            history = (self.embeddings[self.category](batch['history_category']) +\n                       self.embeddings[self.brand](batch['history_brand']) +\n                       self.action(batch['history_type']) + self.recency(ages))\n            # [B,4,L] @ [B,L,d] avoids an expanded [B,4,L,d] temporary.\n            # Empty groups divide by one and have an exactly zero numerator.\n            pools = torch.bmm(membership, history) / sizes.clamp_min(1).unsqueeze(-1)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read paper:din --start-line 225 --lines 65'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read paper:din --start-line 225 --lines 65'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:din\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06978v4\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:5fee25f33f375e51b3ee9417d376ff9dbe2ea3628c932a7679ddfeb0feaaade2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"83fb9eeca31e2fa3c557b6f08b893ad0018cefb33da6e00952136b9ce17e607e\",\n  \"total_lines\": 831,\n  \"start_line\": 225,\n  \"next_line\": 290,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"225: As the inputs are high dimensional binary vectors, embedding layer is used to transform them into low dimensional dense representations.\\n226: For the i-th feature group of 𝒕i\\\\bm{t}_{i}, let Wi=[w1i,…,wji,…,wKii]∈ℝD×Ki\\\\mathrm{W}^{i}=[w_{1}^{i},...,w_{j}^{i},...,w_{K_{i}}^{i}]\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{D\\\\times K_{i}} represent the i-th embedding dictionary, where wji∈RDw_{j}^{i}\\\\in R^{D} is an embedding vector with dimensionality of DD.\\n227: Embedding operation follows the table lookup mechanism, as illustrated in Fig.2.\\n228: •\\n229: If 𝒕i\\\\bm{t}_{i} is one-hot vector with j-th element 𝒕i​[j]=1\\\\bm{t}_{i}[j]=1, the embedded representation of 𝒕i\\\\bm{t}_{i} is a single embedding vector 𝒆i=wji\\\\bm{e}_{i}=w_{j}^{i}.\\n230: •\\n231: If 𝒕i\\\\bm{t}_{i} is multi-hot vector with 𝒕i​[j]=1​for​j∈{i1,i2,…,ik}\\\\bm{t}_{i}[j]=1\\\\penalty\\\\ \\\\text{for}\\\\penalty\\\\ j\\\\in\\\\{i_{1},i_{2},...,i_{k}\\\\}, the embedded representation of 𝒕i\\\\bm{t}_{i} is a list of embedding vectors: {𝒆i1,𝒆i2,…​𝒆ik}={wi1i,wi2i,…​wiki}\\\\{\\\\bm{e}_{i_{1}},\\\\bm{e}_{i_{2}},...\\\\bm{e}_{i_{k}}\\\\}=\\\\{w_{i_{1}}^{i},w_{i_{2}}^{i},...w_{i_{k}}^{i}\\\\}.\\n232: Pooling layer and Concat layer\\n233: Notice that different users have different numbers of behaviors. Thus the number of non-zero values for multi-hot behavioral feature vector 𝒕i\\\\bm{t}_{i} varies across instances, causing the lengths of the corresponding list of embedding vectors to be variable.\\n234: As fully connected networks can only handle fixed-length inputs, it is a common practice (et al., 2016a; Covington\\n235: et al., 2016) to transform the list of embedding vectors via a pooling layer to get a fixed-length vector:\\n236: (1)\\n237: 𝒆i=pooling​(𝒆i1,𝒆i2,…​𝒆ik).\\\\bm{e}_{i}=\\\\text{pooling}(\\\\bm{e}_{i_{1}},\\\\bm{e}_{i_{2}},...\\\\bm{e}_{i_{k}}).\\n238: Two most commonly used pooling layers are sum pooling and average pooling, which apply element-wise sum/average operations to the list of embedding vectors.\\n239: Both embedding and pooling layers operate in a group-wise manner, mapping the original sparse features into multiple fixed-length representation vectors.\\n240: Then all the vectors are concatenated together to obtain the overall representation vector for the instance.\\n241: MLP\\n242: Given the concatenated dense representation vector, fully connected layers are used to learn the combination of features automatically.\\n243: Recently developed methods (et al., 2016a; Guo\\n244: et al., 2017; et al., 2016b) focus on designing structures of MLP for better information extraction.\\n245: Loss\\n246: The objective function used in base model is the negative log-likelihood function defined as:\\n247: (2)\\n248: L=−1N∑(𝒙,y)∈𝒮(ylogp(𝒙)+(1−y)log(1−p(𝒙))),L=-\\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{S}}(y\\\\log p(\\\\bm{x})+(1-y)\\\\log(1-p(\\\\bm{x}))),\\n249: where 𝒮\\\\mathcal{S} is the training set of size NN, with 𝒙\\\\bm{x} as the input of the network and y∈{0,1}y\\\\in\\\\{0,1\\\\} as the label, p⁡(𝒙)p(\\\\bm{x}) is the output of the network after the softmax layer, representing the predicted probability of sample 𝒙\\\\bm{x} being clicked.\\n250: 4.3. The structure of Deep Interest Network\\n251: Among all those features of Table 1, user behavior features are critically important and play key roles in modeling user interests in the scenario of e-commerce applications.\\n252: Base model obtains a fixed-length representation vector of user interests by pooling all the embedding vectors over the user behavior feature group, as Eq.(1). This representation vector stays the same for a given user, in regardless of what candidate ads are.\\n253: In this way, the user representation vector with a limited dimension will be a bottleneck to express user’s diverse interests.\\n254: To make it capable enough, an easy method is to expand the dimension of embedding vector, which unfortunately will increase the size of learning parameters heavily.\\n255: It will lead to overfitting under limited training data and add the burden of computation and storage, which may not be tolerated for an industrial online system.\\n256: Is there an elegant way to represent user’s diverse interests in one vector under limited dimension?\\n257: The local activation characteristic of user interests gives us inspiration to design a novel model named deep interest network(DIN).\\n258: Imagine when the young mother mentioned above in section 3 visits the e-commerce site, she finds the displayed new handbag cute and clicks it.\\n259: Let’s dissect the driving force of click action.\\n260: The displayed ad hits the related interests of this young mother by soft-searching her historical behaviors and finding that she had browsed similar goods of tote bag and leather handbag recently.\\n261: In other words, behaviors related to displayed ad greatly contribute to the click action.\\n262: DIN simulates this process by paying attention to the representation of locally activated interests w.r.t. given ad.\\n263: Instead of expressing all user’s diverse interests with the same vector, DIN adaptively calculate the representation vector of user interests by taking into consideration the relevance of historical behaviors w.r.t. candidate ad.\\n264: This representation vector varies over different ads.\\n265: The right part of Fig.2 illustrates the architecture of DIN.\\n266: Compared with base model, DIN introduces a novel designed local activation unit and maintains the other structures the same. Specifically, activation units are applied on the user behavior features, which performs as a weighted sum pooling to adaptively calculate user representation 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} given a candidate ad AA, as shown in Eq.(3)\\n267: (3)\\n268: 𝒗U(A)=f(𝒗A,𝒆1,𝒆2,..,𝒆H)=∑j=1Ha⁡(𝒆j,𝒗A)​𝒆j=∑j=1H𝒘j​𝒆j,\\\\displaystyle\\\\begin{split}\\\\bm{v}_{U}(A)=f(\\\\bm{v}_{A},\\\\bm{e}_{1},\\\\bm{e}_{2},..,\\\\bm{e}_{H})&=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{H}a(\\\\bm{e}_{j},\\\\bm{v}_{A})\\\\bm{e}_{j}=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{H}\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\bm{e}_{j},\\\\end{split}\\n269: where {𝒆1,𝒆2,…,𝒆H}\\\\{\\\\bm{e}_{1},\\\\bm{e}_{2},...,\\\\bm{e}_{H}\\\\} is the list of embedding vectors of behaviors of user UU with length of HH, 𝒗A\\\\bm{v}_{A} is the embedding vector of ad AA. In this way, 𝒗U​(A)\\\\bm{v}_{U}(A) varies over different ads. a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) is a feed-forward network with output as the activation weight, as illustrated in Fig.2. Apart from the two input embedding vectors, a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) adds the out product of them to feed into the subsequent network, which is an explicit knowledge to help relevance modeling.\\n270: Local activation unit of Eq.(3) shares similar ideas with attention methods which are developed in NMT task(Bahdanau\\n271: et al., 2015).\\n272: However different from traditional attention method, the constraint of ∑iwi=1\\\\sum_{i}w_{i}=1 is relaxed in Eq.(3), aiming to reserve the intensity of user interests.\\n273: That is, normalization with softmax on the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) is abandoned.\\n274: Instead, value of ∑iwi\\\\sum_{i}w_{i} is treated as an approximation of the intensity of activated user interests to some degree.\\n275: For example, if one user’s historical behaviors contain 90% clothes and 10% electronics.\\n276: Given two candidate ads of T-shirt and phone, T-shirt activates most of the historical behaviors belonging to clothes and may get larger value of 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} (higher intensity of interest) than phone.\\n277: Traditional attention methods lose the resolution on the numerical scale of 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} by normalizing of the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot).\\n278: We have tried LSTM to model user historical behavior data in the sequential manner.\\n279: But it shows no improvement.\\n280: Different from text which is under the constraint of grammar in NLP task, the sequence of user historical behaviors may contain multiple concurrent interests.\\n281: Rapid jumping and sudden ending over these interests causes the sequence data of user behaviors to seem to be noisy.\\n282: A possible direction is to design special structures to model such data in a sequence way.\\n283: We leave it for future research.\\n284: 5. Training Techniques\\n285: In the advertising system in Alibaba, numbers of goods and users scale up to hundreds of millions.\\n286: Practically, training industrial deep networks with large scale sparse input features is of great challenge.\\n287: In this section, we introduce two important techniques which are proven to be helpful in practice.\\n288: 5.1. Mini-batch Aware Regularization\\n289: Overfitting is a critical challenge for training industrial networks.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
